qedit('nsec-threats',1,'prompt','An attacker passively captures nearby Wi-Fi traffic to try to read another user\'s data. Which security property is targeted?','Capture is not guaranteed to include every frame or reveal encrypted plaintext.')
qedit('nsec-threats',1,'options.1.explanation','Correct. Passive eavesdropping targets confidentiality. Capturing ciphertext alone does not necessarily reveal application plaintext.','Distinguish attacking confidentiality from successfully breaking encryption.')
qreplace('nsec-threats',1,'workedExample','5. What is lost is secrecy: the attacker reads data meant for someone else. That is **confidentiality**.','5. The target is secrecy: reading data meant for someone else would violate **confidentiality**. Correctly encrypted ciphertext need not reveal its plaintext.','Do not equate packet capture with decryption.')
qedit('nsec-threats',7,'prompt','For an incoming SYN that the server handles using a SYN cookie, what changes compared with retaining a conventional half-open queue entry?','Enabling cookies may only enable overflow fallback; scope to a cookie-handled packet.')
qedit('nsec-firewalls',5,'options.0.explanation','Correct. Security groups have no explicit deny rules. For traffic they filter, unmatched new traffic is denied; documented exceptions such as Amazon-provided DNS are not blocked by security groups.','Account for documented traffic exceptions.')
qedit('nsec-vpns',4,'options.0.explanation','The size-dependent failure pattern with blocked ICMP points more directly to PMTU discovery. Crypto resource exhaustion is another possible failure mode, so the symptom alone is not proof.','Slow cryptography cannot be categorically excluded as a cause of failure.')
qedit('nsec-vpns',4,'options.3.explanation','An idle NAT mapping timeout is not inherently packet-size dependent, and outbound traffic can create a new mapping. Keepalives do not fix a PMTU black hole.','Expired NAT does not necessarily break entire tunnel permanently.')
qreplace('nsec-vpns',5,'workedExample','1   yes     1   -2..1\n2   yes     2   -1..2','1   yes     1    1..1\n2   yes     2    1..2','Only positive sequence numbers are admissible in this toy model.')
qreplace('nsec-vpns',5,'workedExample','the window (top − 3 .. top)','the window (max(1, top − 3) .. top)','Clip the toy window to valid positive sequence numbers.')
qedit('nsec-vpns',7,'options.3.explanation','Correct. WireGuard uses UDP and a configurable port. NAT and firewall traversal depend on their policies and mapping state; UDP alone does not guarantee reachability.','Remove false implication that a UDP port automatically passes NATs.')
qedit('nsec-wireless',1,'options.1.text','Radio signals can extend beyond controlled premises, exposing traffic to suitably equipped nearby receivers','Being in range does not guarantee receiving every frame.')
qedit('nsec-wireless',1,'workedExample','Radio signals can pass beyond walls and physical access controls, so a nearby observer may capture traffic without joining the network. Wired traffic can also be exposed through taps, compromised equipment or misconfiguration; physical premises alone do not supply cryptographic confidentiality or integrity. Link-layer protection and application TLS address different parts of this threat model.','Do not treat Ethernet physical security as absolute or imply walls cryptographically protect packets.')
qreplace('nsec-wireless',6,'workedExample','The client, following the standard, installs the PTK again on the second M3.','A vulnerable client installs the PTK again on the retransmitted M3.','Not every implementation reinstalls keys.')
qreplace('nsec-wireless',6,'workedExample','the transmit packet number (the CCMP nonce)','the transmit packet number (a component of the CCMP nonce)','CCMP nonce contains more than the packet number.')
qedit('nsec-wireless',10,'options.1.explanation','Correct. Disable or revoke the individual credential, then terminate existing sessions or ensure reauthentication and revocation checks enforce the change. Disabling an account need not instantly remove cached keys or active sessions.','Separate future authentication denial from existing session revocation.')
qedit('nsec-wireless',10,'options.4.explanation','Incorrect. The four-way handshake still runs using a PMK derived from the EAP/AAA key hierarchy; RADIUS commonly transports keying material rather than mandating a direct PMK field.','Avoid conflating transported MSK material with PMK derivation.')
qedit('nsec-tls',10,'prompt','Consider full TLS 1.3 handshakes using certificates for mutual authentication between internal services. A mesh may manage this, or a trusted load balancer may terminate the client session. Which statements are true? Select all that apply.','CertificateVerify is not repeated in PSK-resumed handshakes; scope to full certificate handshakes.')
qedit('nsec-tls',11,'options.2.text','Only 3 certificates cover the whole 365-day interval','Original distractor said three renewals, which plus initial issuance actually gives four certificates.')
qedit('nsec-tls',11,'options.2.explanation','Three 100-day certificates cover at most 300 days. Four certificates are needed: one initial issuance and three replacements in the ideal non-overlapping model.','Distinguish certificates from renewals.')
